course: dictionary
lesson: 4824
title: Hurling: one word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. To pick the right one, look at the words around it. In «A rugby union team has 15 players on the pitch, and so does a hurling team», the clues are players and pitch, so hurling is a sport here.
explain: Now read «hurling stones» in a story. There is no team or pitch, and stones are being thrown hard and far. Here, hurling means throwing something hard and far, often angrily.
explain: Check your choice by putting your meaning back into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have probably got it right. Hurling can be a noun (the sport) or a verb (the -ing form of hurl).
fact: Hurling is a fast Irish team sport played with a wooden stick called a hurley and a small ball called a sliotar.
fact: A hurling team has 15 players on the pitch, the same as a rugby union team.
fact: Camogie is the version of hurling played by women and girls.
fact: Hurl means to throw something hard and far, often angrily, so «hurling stones» means throwing stones hard.
fact: Informally, hurling can also mean being sick, but this is slang, so use it with care.
exercise: «Hurling is a fast Irish team sport played with a wooden stick.» What kind of thing is hurling here? | *A fast team sport from Ireland | A type of painting | A quiet game for one player | A kind of dance
exercise: «The boy was hurling stones into the lake.» What is he doing? | Sleeping on the stones | Painting the stones blue | Counting the stones carefully | *Throwing the stones hard and far
exercise: «A rugby union team has 15 players on the pitch, and so does a hurling team.» How many players does a hurling team have on the pitch? | 18 | 11 | *15 | 13
test: «She was hurling insults at him, and he went red with shame.» What does hurling mean here? | *Throwing angry words at him | Ice skating across the room | Singing softly to him | Cooking him a meal
test: Ciara says camogie is a kind of bread. What is camogie? | A type of Irish bread | A kind of train | *A version of hurling played by women and girls | A school lunch
exam: «The hurling match was played on a sunny afternoon, and the crowd cheered.» Using the context, what does hurling mean here? | A type of weather | *A fast Irish team sport played with a stick and a ball | A kind of music | Throwing something hard and far in anger
